  "worked_solution": "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1)$, can be found by computing $\u2207f(\\frac{3}{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{\u00a0}i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(x+3y-2xy)\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =(1-2y)\\text{\u00a0}i+(3-2x)\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\ \u2207f(\\frac{3}{2},1) & =(1-2(1))\\text{\u00a0}i+(3-2(\\frac{3}{2}))\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $\u2207f(\\frac{3}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2207f(\\frac{3}{2},1)}{\u2016\u2207f(\\frac{3}{2},1)\u2016} & =\\frac{-i}{1}=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$",
